## Part A — The pattern table  [40 Points]

One regular expression per intent. Each must catch all three of its own sample phrasings, catch
nothing belonging to another intent, and be wrapped in `\b`.

**The ordering trap.** `"i want to book a study room"` contains the word *book*, which also belongs
to `borrow`. Since the first match wins, `rooms` must come first. Decide the whole order
deliberately — you explain it in the README.
# PART A - one pattern per intent.
#
# Rules are tried in order and the FIRST match wins, so the most specific intent
# goes first. "i want to book a study room" contains "book", which also belongs
# to borrow - so rooms must be checked before borrow.
#
# Wrap every pattern in \b so that it matches whole words only.
#
# ORDER USED (and why):
#   1. rooms   - "book a study room" contains "book", which borrow also uses.
#   2. hours   - "time"/"open" never appear in another intent's phrasings.
#   3. renew   - "i want to extend my book" contains "book", so renew must also
#                come BEFORE borrow.
#   4. borrow  - broad keyword "book(s)", so it is checked late.
#   5. fines, 6. printing - their keywords do not overlap with anything else.

RULES = [
    ("rooms",    r"\b(rooms?|reserve|reserving|reservation)\b"),
    ("hours",    r"\b(open|opens|opening|hours|closing|closes|time)\b"),
    ("renew",    r"\b(renew|renews|renewing|renewal|renewals|extend|extension)\b"),
    ("borrow",   r"\b(borrow|borrowing|borrowed|books?|keep)\b"),
    ("fines",    r"\b(fines?|overdue|charges?|late|penalty)\b"),
    ("printing", r"\b(print|prints|printing|printer|pages?|photocopy|photocopying|copies)\b"),
]


def match(message):
    """PROVIDED. Return the tag of the first pattern that matches, or "fallback"."""
    text = normalise(message)
    for tag, pattern in RULES:
        if re.search(pattern, text):
            return tag
    return "fallback"


for m in ["what time do you open", "can i renew", "i want to book a study room",
          "do you have a bookshop"]:
    print(f"{m!r:36} -> {match(m)}")
